{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1499743-opinions-on-typical-composition-of-a-european-court","id":1499743,"title":"Opinions on Typical Composition of a European Court?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-15T06:16:53-08:00","id":1499743,"title":"Opinions on Typical Composition of a European Court?","content":"I'm wondering what the typical composition of a European city vampire court would be, especially for larger cities like London and Paris. I realize that there are canon Princes and various elders, Primogens, and Justicars, etc and I'm assuming that they account for maybe as much as the top third of a court? Or is it more or less?<br /> <br /> So my current assumptions are that in any European city that the population contains:<br /> <br /> - about a third neonates (i.e. starting PC power level) formed into various coteries<br /> - another third made up of mostly ancilla or weaker elders, some in coteries but mostly individuals<br /> - a top third elders and named/canon vampires which includes the Prince/Bishop and other high functionaries like the Sheriff, Scourge, etc<br /> <br /> plus<br /> <br /> - a smattering of fledglings attached to various elders or ancilla<br /> - approximately one ghoul per vampire, although most of these would never be seen at court unless they are being groomed for embrace<br /> - a very small number of 'others' - independents, visitors, and other supernaturals who are on good terms with vampires<br /> <br /> Does that make sense proportion wise or do you think that there should be more neonates and fewer elders/ancilla, or some other form of distribution? and what difference is there amongst the Sects?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-15T19:12:46-08:00","id":1499825,"title":"","content":"Personally, I would go for a more pyramidal structure, with about roughly double the number of each group compared to the one on the top.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-15T20:21:20-08:00","id":1499827,"title":"","content":"In a general sense, Europe was always supposed to have a lot more elders than the Americas, and them usually being a bit more conservative about new embracees. So, you would tend to skew towards a Kindred population that was a lot older and of lower generations. However, I've generally been of the opinion that WW2 would have disrupted this, with a number of elders (as well as vampires in general) being destroyed and the vacuum having to be filled by new childer. What you probably end up with are a lot of elders from the Roman and Medieval eras, a smaller number of ancilla from the Renaissance to just before WW1, and then a sudden surge of newbies from the latter half of the 20th century. (If the PCs are all supposed to be new young vampires, then they may be the majority of the city/region's new embraces this century. At least among the legitimate Camarilla court.) You probably also have more than a few lineages/dynasties in cities, where prominent elders or even Methuselah have childer, grandchilder, or even great grandchilder serving them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}